1
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:05,780
We're not gonna get anywhere until we get past your denial of the problem.

2
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:08,340
What problem? You see, that's what...

3
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:12,800
Isn't it odd, the only people who say I have a problem are my wife, my kids, my

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:14,960
boss, and those fair -weather friends of mine.

5
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:16,420
Well, what does that tell you?

6
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:18,220
That they got a problem.

7
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:20,800
All right, see you next week then.

8
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:22,540
Sure, but I don't know why.

9
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:34,380
Maggie, it's Bill at the New York Times.

10
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:37,420
I'm afraid you're overqualified for an entry -level position.

11
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:40,420
Thanks anyway. Who needs the New York Times?

12
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:41,760
You're just a fish wrapper.

13
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:45,920
This is Thelma at Channel 19 News.

14
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:48,100
Mr. Sidlovich is confirming your 3 p.m.

15
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:50,560
interview tomorrow. TV news?

16
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:51,740
Not bad, Maggie.

17
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:54,580
This is Thelma again.

18
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:56,760
I'm sorry, we have to cancel that interview.

19
00:00:57,420 --> 00:00:59,800
Fools. That's why you're in TV news.

20
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:04,660
Maggie, this is Susan again.

21
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:07,120
How about returning your old boss's calls?

22
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:10,060
I've got something you'll be very interested in.

23
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:14,840
All right. Hey, how'd the interview go?

24
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:17,680
Well, the editor talked to me for a solid hour.

25
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:20,200
Well, it sounds as if he liked what he heard.

26
00:01:20,540 --> 00:01:21,760
Sounds like a smart guy.

27
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:23,340
He didn't hire me.

28
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:29,320
He's scum. Jason, if I hear one more rejection today, I'm gonna scream.

29
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:34,020
Any messages? Yeah, well, yes, yes.

30
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:38,060
Jason? Bill. Bill called from the New York Times.

31
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:41,040
You came so close, you came very, very close.

32
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:46,040
And, uh... Channel 19 called.

33
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:48,900
Oh, did they confirm my interview?

34
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:51,060
Yes. And no.

35
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:53,860
What? Well, they had to cancel.

36
00:01:53,860 --> 00:01:57,340
Oh, so he's turning me down before he meets me.

37
00:01:57,340 --> 00:01:59,280
Nice. Save everybody time.

38
00:01:59,360 --> 00:01:59,800
No, no, no.

39
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:01,060
He just got busy today.

40
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:04,000
Something about a member of Congress and twin strippers.

41
00:02:04,420 --> 00:02:05,720
I didn't get the details.

42
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:09,560
Jason, you're so...sick.

43
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:13,800
Yeah. Anyway, I say the best for the last.

44
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:17,700
Wait till you hear Susan called, and she has something very...

45
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:19,240
I'll be very interested in.

46
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:21,640
I know she's been calling for a solid week.

47
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:23,760
Well, you see, this is a good thing.

48
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:24,540
No, it isn't.

49
00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:25,660
No, it isn't.

50
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:28,300
No. That's why I saved it for the last.

51
00:02:28,620 --> 00:02:32,580
Well, Jason, she probably wants me to come crawling back to my old job, hat in hand,

52
00:02:32,740 --> 00:02:35,260
virtually admitting it was a mistake to quit in the first place.

53
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:37,780
Well, at least call Channel 19.

54
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:38,780
Reset that interview.

55
00:02:39,060 --> 00:02:40,640
Oh, Jason, what's the point?

56
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:42,360
Honey, you're just confused.

57
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:44,560
You're thinking, here you are, out of work.

58
00:02:44,780 --> 00:02:46,880
You made a mistake by quitting that job.

59
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:48,620
You're feeling a little over the hill.

60
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:51,380
But there's another hill, Maggie.

61
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:55,040
There's another. There's a huge hill out there, a mountain bigger.

62
00:02:56,900 --> 00:02:59,200
When she thinks about that, she'll feel better.

63
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:01,660
Show me that smile, look at me.

64
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:03,580
Ooh, show me that smile.

65
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:07,520
Don't waste another minute on your crying.

66
00:03:07,780 --> 00:03:09,820
We're nowhere near the end.

67
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:11,060
We're nowhere near the end.

68
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:15,040
The fest is ready to begin.

69
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:25,260
Oh, as long as we got each other, we got the world spinning right in our hands.

70
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:33,120
Baby, you and me, we gotta be the luckiest dreamers who never quit dreaming.

71
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:36,180
As long as we keep on giving,

72
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:41,020
we can take anything that comes our way.

73
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:51,580
Baby, when I shine all the time We got each other sharing the laughter and the love

74
00:04:00,860 --> 00:04:05,380
I can't believe you wanted me to scrunch down on the floor of your car all the way

75
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:07,240
home. And I can't believe you wouldn't do it.

76
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:08,660
I have an image to maintain.

77
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:11,440
Yeah. Did you hear me complaining?

78
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:15,020
Oh, well, maybe I could just wear a bag over my head.

79
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:16,540
That's not a bad idea.

80
00:04:16,740 --> 00:04:17,860
Cut some little eyeballs and...

81
00:04:17,860 --> 00:04:20,080
All right. Guys.

82
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:22,500
Mom! No, no, your mom has enough on her mind.

83
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:23,680
Don't give her any more headaches.

84
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:26,920
She's been having a rough time these days, and you all know why.

85
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:35,080
Why? Well, because mom's been bombing out in the job market, and she feels like dog

86
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:36,900
meat. I didn't know that.

87
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:38,420
Ben, where have you been?

88
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:39,940
On the floor of Mike's car.

89
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:43,120
Why would Mom feel bad about not working?

90
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:45,800
You know, now that you mention it, Benny, I never even understood why Mom went to

91
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:46,640
work in the first place.

92
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:49,500
You know, I mean, she's got a pretty good job just hanging out here at home.

93
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:53,240
You know, I mean, I wish some guy had come along and married me

94
00:04:55,020 --> 00:04:58,120
and... pay my bills and buy me pretty dresses.

95
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:02,320
You know what I mean.

96
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:05,940
I should have known you two wouldn't understand.

97
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:07,100
I mean, how could you?

98
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:10,840
Hey, look, if you're so smart, why don't you tell us what Mom needs to get a job?

99
00:05:11,520 --> 00:05:13,480
Okay. To feel vital.

100
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:18,000
To be a productive, self -reliant cog in the machinery of society.

101
00:05:18,580 --> 00:05:20,280
To forge her own identity.

102
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:23,060
Just like I thought.

103
00:05:23,060 --> 00:05:24,020
She doesn't have a clue.

104
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:28,940
Honey, you coming to bed soon?

105
00:05:29,160 --> 00:05:30,120
Oh, wait a minute, sweetheart.

106
00:05:30,140 --> 00:05:31,720
I have to finish retyping my resume.

107
00:05:32,120 --> 00:05:33,720
Well, it looked fine to me before.

108
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:35,120
Are you kidding?

109
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:37,820
It finally dawned on me what was wrong with it.

110
00:05:37,960 --> 00:05:40,040
What? Well, it's obvious.

111
00:05:40,260 --> 00:05:41,480
Any fool can see it.

112
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:43,780
I don't see it.

113
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:47,920
My name. It's in all capital letters.

114
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:51,600
So? Well, don't you think that's kind of self -important?

115
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:55,280
It's 1230 in the morning.

116
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:57,960
Maybe you should do this when you're thinking a little more clearly.

117
00:05:58,500 --> 00:05:59,860
Not that you're not now.

118
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:02,340
Honey, I can't do it.

119
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:05,700
I've been walking on eggs around here for three weeks now and it's just not working.

120
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:10,000
I gotta be honest with you, changing your resume because your name is in capitals is

121
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,560
nuts. There, I've said it.

122
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:17,340
Well then why do you think I haven't been hired?

123
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:19,480
Well now that's an excellent question, Maggie.

124
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:21,280
That's the essential question here.

125
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:25,600
I think our time would be very productively spent if we just thought about that for

126
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:27,500
a while and discussed all the possible reasons.

127
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,800
Maybe I should triple space this.

128
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:33,320
No, no, no, honey, no, forget typing.

129
00:06:33,740 --> 00:06:35,460
No, deal with the question here.

130
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:37,300
Why do you think you haven't been hired?

131
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:42,040
Possibly some men are intimidated by a talented, sexy blonde.

132
00:06:43,360 --> 00:06:44,660
Do you believe that?

133
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:47,560
I'd like to.

134
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:50,440
I think you're losing your perspective.

135
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:52,540
No, I am losing my mind.

136
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:54,500
All I want is a job where I feel needed.

137
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:57,020
Carol was right.

138
00:06:57,760 --> 00:06:59,040
How could that be?

139
00:07:00,680 --> 00:07:02,520
Look, I'll be up in a few minutes, okay?

140
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:05,880
Okay. I love you.

141
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:06,780
Love you, too.

142
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:13,860
Mom? Betty, what are you doing up?

143
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:15,520
I was worried about you.

144
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:17,240
Oh, you were?

145
00:07:18,120 --> 00:07:21,800
Mom, I just want you to know that I'll love you even if no one on Earth will hire

146
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:25,500
you. Why, thank you, Benjamin.

147
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:27,780
Mom, you don't need a job to feel good.

148
00:07:27,780 --> 00:07:29,980
I mean, I've never had a job and I feel great.

149
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:34,060
You know, I've never looked at it that way.

150
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:38,020
You should. I mean, I eat, I watch TV, I do my homework.

151
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:39,740
Sometimes I don't even do my homework.

152
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:42,740
Sounds very nice.

153
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:46,720
It is. So, are you done worrying now?

154
00:07:47,740 --> 00:07:49,540
Well, let's put it this way, Ben.

155
00:07:49,900 --> 00:07:51,740
I'm very glad we had this talk.

156
00:07:53,940 --> 00:07:56,980
You know, you'd feel even better if we split a wedge of pie.

157
00:07:56,980 --> 00:08:02,000
Sure. Not bad.

158
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:04,640
Oh, here's your breakfast, Michael.

159
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:06,500
What am I supposed to do with this?

160
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:08,860
Eat it. Pardon me?

161
00:08:09,560 --> 00:08:11,500
Eat, sit, enjoy.

162
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:13,300
Oh, a hot breakfast.

163
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:14,420
What an interesting idea.

164
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:18,280
Morning. How you want them, Carol?

165
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:20,020
Scramble over easy or sunny side up?

166
00:08:20,140 --> 00:08:21,880
Oh, I usually just have juice.

167
00:08:22,140 --> 00:08:23,240
Mm -hmm. Not anymore.

168
00:08:25,380 --> 00:08:26,540
What's going on?

169
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:27,680
Oh, she's lost her mind.

170
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:28,460
Pass the syrup.

171
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:31,880
Morning. Hi, Betty.

172
00:08:32,020 --> 00:08:32,840
Pancakes or eggs?

173
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:33,740
Sausage or ham?

174
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:42,460
Sure. Morning. And now that we're all together, I have an announcement to make.

175
00:08:43,140 --> 00:08:47,060
I had an enlightening talk last night with a very wise man.

176
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:53,740
And he helped me realize that I've put too much importance on this job hunt, and

177
00:08:53,740 --> 00:08:55,740
it's getting in the way of more important things.

178
00:08:55,740 --> 00:09:00,280
So I've decided to just be happy taking care of the family I love.

179
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:01,560
You got any more flapjacks?

180
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:03,940
You're a pig.

181
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:08,040
I'm so glad you had that talk with that very wise man.

182
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:09,540
And he loves you very much.

183
00:09:09,860 --> 00:09:11,940
Yeah, even if you didn't give him a piece of pie.

184
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:15,780
Huh? So let's get this house in order.

185
00:09:16,120 --> 00:09:20,360
I've got a list here of chores that have been neglected for a very long time.

186
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:22,400
This is some kind of joke, right?

187
00:09:23,220 --> 00:09:25,960
No, Mike, if I'm gonna be home, I'm gonna do a good job.

188
00:09:27,240 --> 00:09:29,860
Okay, who's the wise guy who spoke to mom last night?

189
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:31,940
Well, your mom's right.

190
00:09:31,980 --> 00:09:33,340
Everybody has to pull their own weight.

191
00:09:33,720 --> 00:09:35,000
Well, thank you, sweetheart.

192
00:09:35,620 --> 00:09:37,660
I rented you a rototiller.

193
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:40,720
It'll be delivered this afternoon.

194
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:44,820
Oh, boy. Oh, that's your bus, Ben.

195
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:46,480
Mike, Carol, you should go.

196
00:09:46,620 --> 00:09:47,000
You'll be late.

197
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:48,320
Don't forget your lunches.

198
00:09:49,020 --> 00:09:55,400
Come on, I'm only gonna be gone for one day.

199
00:09:57,980 --> 00:10:02,960
So in the middle of my summation, I look up and the jury foreman is asleep.

200
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:05,100
Can you imagine?

201
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:17,520
Doctor? Doctor! Well, I guess juries are not the only people who find me boring.

202
00:10:17,520 --> 00:10:19,580
No, this was completely unprofessional.

203
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:20,500
I'm very sorry.

204
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:23,940
I had a very big, enormous breakfast, and I didn't even want it.

205
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:24,880
I had sausages.

206
00:10:25,140 --> 00:10:26,100
I had pancakes.

207
00:10:27,860 --> 00:10:29,160
Anyway, I'm sorry.

208
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:33,120
I'll see you next week, and no charge for this session, obviously.

209
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:40,800
Obviously. Oh, no.

210
00:10:41,780 --> 00:10:44,560
Oh, you spoiled the surprise.

211
00:10:44,560 --> 00:10:48,940
Maggie, we have to have a calm, rational talk.

212
00:10:49,580 --> 00:10:51,200
Okay. Get a job!

213
00:10:52,680 --> 00:10:56,100
What? I'm sorry, it's my stomach talking, not my head.

214
00:10:56,420 --> 00:10:58,500
You don't like my cape on, Medley?

215
00:10:59,280 --> 00:11:03,480
My cheeks, I really don't think that you're nearly as happy being at home as you

216
00:11:03,480 --> 00:11:04,060
seem to be.

217
00:11:04,220 --> 00:11:06,580
Oh, of course I'm happy, Jason.

218
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:08,820
Look, three different cheeses.

219
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:14,020
Maggie, I'm very flattered that you took my advice last night.

220
00:11:14,020 --> 00:11:15,720
No, no, no, I didn't take your advice.

221
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:17,220
I took Ben's.

222
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:21,080
Ben's? The very wise man was Ben?

223
00:11:21,560 --> 00:11:22,820
Well, he made a lot of sense.

224
00:11:22,860 --> 00:11:25,060
He said I didn't need a job to be happy.

225
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:27,260
Oh, what does he know about jobs, Maggie?

226
00:11:27,340 --> 00:11:28,200
He's 11 years old.

227
00:11:28,220 --> 00:11:29,540
He's never worked a day in his life.

228
00:11:30,260 --> 00:11:32,100
Well, that was his point.

229
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:35,900
You know, he's just made it easier for me to accept the fact that there was simply

230
00:11:35,900 --> 00:11:37,280
no jobs out there for me.

231
00:11:37,720 --> 00:11:41,000
Honey, the only thing keeping you out of the job market is pride.

232
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:42,300
What? Pride, honey.

233
00:11:42,400 --> 00:11:44,000
There is a job out there for you.

234
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:46,700
Jason, I followed up on every single lead.

235
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:49,020
Except one. How many times has Susan called?

236
00:11:49,320 --> 00:11:51,160
Yeah, about my old job.

237
00:11:51,380 --> 00:11:53,540
Right. What was wrong with your old job?

238
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:55,160
Well, I'll tell you.

239
00:11:55,600 --> 00:11:57,040
It... I'm waiting.

240
00:11:57,820 --> 00:11:58,700
Well, it, uh...

241
00:11:58,700 --> 00:11:59,380
Admit it, honey.

242
00:11:59,380 --> 00:12:02,680
You loved your old job and you found it very rewarding for three years.

243
00:12:02,780 --> 00:12:05,220
Yeah, but... And you were good at it, Maggie.

244
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:09,020
I was pretty good at it, wasn't I?

245
00:12:09,020 --> 00:12:10,320
You still are.

246
00:12:10,320 --> 00:12:12,680
Come on in sitting there waiting for you.

247
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:14,320
What's stopping you?

248
00:12:15,740 --> 00:12:19,280
Gee, I wonder what little Ben would advise.

249
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:28,780
Hello, old desk.

250
00:12:29,860 --> 00:12:31,200
Hello, old chair.

251
00:12:33,900 --> 00:12:40,300
Hi. I'm back.

252
00:12:40,320 --> 00:12:44,180
Maggie! Well, Susan, I figured to heck with my pride.

253
00:12:44,240 --> 00:12:47,320
I owe it to you to hear what you have to say, but if you feel that it's something

254
00:12:47,320 --> 00:12:50,760
that I'd want, then that's good enough for me, and you know how I hate to dick or

